## [ISSUE] Recurring cancel ignores non-organizer host references and repeats work

**Severity:** MEDIUM — other hosts keep all recurring occurrences after cancellation

### The Problem
Inside `for (const reference of bookingCalendarReference)`, the recurring branch iterates `bookingToDelete.user.credentials` (organizer only) and for each `updBooking` uses `updBooking.references.find((ref) => ref.type.includes("_calendar"))` — the first reference only. `reference`/`calendarCredential` resolved for this iteration are unused.

### Theory / Proof
With N calendar references (N hosts), the same organizer deletions run N times (N x credentials x occurrences API calls), while references belonging to host #2's credential are never passed to host #2's calendar.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Non-recurring cancels work (the else branch uses the resolved `calendarCredential`). Recurring collective events leave every occurrence in other hosts' calendars; Google returns 404/410 on the repeated deletes, which are swallowed.

### Research
The non-recurring branch in the same loop is the correct pattern: use `calendarCredential` for the matching reference.

### Recommendation
Move the recurring handling out of the per-reference loop, and inside it iterate all `_calendar` references of each `updBooking`, resolving the credential by `ref.credentialId` (with the same DB fallback):
```ts
for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
  for (const ref of updBooking.references.filter((r) => r.type.includes("_calendar"))) {
    const cred = await resolveCredential(ref.credentialId);
    const cal = cred && (await getCalendar(cred));
    apiDeletes.push(cal?.deleteEvent(ref.uid, evt, ref.externalCalendarId));
  }
}
```

## [ISSUE] loadUsers hides its own validation error behind an opaque 500

**Severity:** LOW — the diagnostic message is lost and client-input errors are reported as server errors

### The Problem
```ts
if (!Array.isArray(dynamicUserList) || dynamicUserList.length === 0) {
  throw new Error("dynamicUserList is not properly defined or empty.");
}
...
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof HttpError || error instanceof Prisma.PrismaClientKnownRequestError) {
    throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 400, message: error.message });
  }
  throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 500, message: "Unable to load users" });
}
```
The plain `Error` thrown in the try block is neither an `HttpError` nor a Prisma error, so it becomes `500 "Unable to load users"` and the specific message is lost. The catch also rewrites any `HttpError` to 400, which would discard its original status code. Nothing in the try throws an `HttpError` today, so that part has no effect yet.

### Theory / Proof
An empty or missing dynamic user list is a client input problem. The error is created a few lines above the catch that swallows it, so the new validation cannot tell the caller anything.

### What happens today vs. what will break
This is not a regression from a 4xx. Before the PR, an empty list also ended in a non-HttpError server error: `No available users found.` at handleNewBooking.ts:429, or an `organizerUser.id` access on undefined at :921. The `eventTypeUser.notFound` check is skipped because dynamic events have `userId: 0` (packages/lib/defaultEvents.ts:92). The PR adds an explicit check but then throws away its message.

### Research
Elsewhere in this handler, input errors are raised with `new HttpError({ statusCode: 400, ... })` directly.

### Recommendation
```ts
throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 400, message: "dynamicUserList is not properly defined or empty." });
...
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof HttpError) throw error;
  if (error instanceof Prisma.PrismaClientKnownRequestError) throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 400, message: error.message });
  throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 500, message: "Unable to load users" });
}
```
